{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Sanctify yourselves for tomorrow, etc.</b> The reason He did not give it until tomorrow is according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Sifrei here), who expounded the word ״הִתְקַדְּשׁוּ״, and this is their language: ״Prepare yourselves for punishment,״ end quote. Therefore HaShem did not wish to give it to them immediately and at once, but rather informed them that what He is giving them in the giving of the meat is punishment, and warned them and extended the time for them and said until <b>tomorrow</b> — and in this they would have the night for soul-searching; perhaps they would return in repentance and HaShem would relent concerning the evil, for HaShem does not desire to destroy His people." "<b>And you shall eat meat.</b> He said <b>וַאֲכַלְתֶּם</b> with the addition of the vav according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Sifrei), who said to them: prepare yourselves, etc. On this basis it is fitting to say ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם,״ for it communicates an additional matter beyond the first — ״Sanctify yourselves for punishment,״ and further: <b>and you shall eat meat.</b>" "<b>Because you wept, etc.</b> This implies that HaShem is indignant about the weeping, and one must know what wrong there is in the weeping on its own. And it appears that the reason for the indignation is because the weeping indicates an absolute deficiency in the negation of [proper] perception, for if not so, they should have asked of HaShem like a son asking his father for whatever his soul desires; and from the fact that they wept, it indicates that the matter is decided among them — that HaShem, as it were (heaven forbid), lacks the power to grant their request. And this is what Scripture precisely says: <b>Who will feed us</b> — to say, there is no one who can do this thing. And so you will find that the Sages, of blessed memory (Sifrei), said that Israel said: ״He has no power,״ etc., and this is heresy regarding the omnipotence of the Holy One, blessed is He. And see the verse that follows this one." "<b>In the ears of HaShem.</b> Meaning, they did not hesitate to speak these words, even though HaShem dwells among them, and the speakers feel and know that the One who hears dwells among them — and in this there is great brazenness, exceedingly so, to speak words of heresy in the ears of HaShem. And see the verse after this one:" "<b>Because it was better for us, etc.</b> One must know the intent of the verse's saying <b>כִּי כִּי</b> [because because]; also, it has no proper measure in the intensity of the language as heard. Perhaps the verse informs us that the lust for meat grew so overpowering that they chose Egypt in order to eat meat there. Further, the verse may be explained in this manner: <b>Because you wept in the ears of HaShem, saying</b> — meaning, the reason for the weeping that you voiced in the ears of HaShem was ״Who will feed us meat״; this was the content of the statement. But the holy spirit, examining their hearts, knows that their ultimate desire is that Egypt was better for them, for they choose the land of Egypt over dwelling in the abode of the Shekhinah. And therefore the anger of HaShem burned against them and He said: prepare yourselves for punishment. And in this you will find satisfaction in HaShem's statement, which He said at the end: <b>Because you have rejected HaShem who is among you</b>, etc. — and we do not find that this was mentioned in their words; according to what we have explained, it fits properly. Also precisely indicated is His saying <b>you have rejected et HaShem</b> with the inclusion of the word את, for the intent is to say that they rejected being with HaShem who dwells among them, and they crave the land of Egypt." "<b>And HaShem will give you meat and you shall eat.</b> One needs to know why He repeated the things He said in the preceding verse. Further, that He was not satisfied with the statement ״וְנָתַן״ etc. and said <b>and you shall eat</b> — but is it not self-evident that they would eat what is given to them? And it appears that Scripture intends to tell them that even though He said ״sanctify yourselves for tomorrow״ etc., which we explained to mean that they should return in repentance and there would be no punishment, and therefore He gave them time until tomorrow — all this would be before the giving of the meat to them; but after He gives them the meat, remorse and repentance will not avail them not to eat it. And this is the meaning of <b>and HaShem will give you meat</b> — then <b>and you shall eat</b>: you are obligated to eat, by compulsion. And by way of remez, He repeated to say so to hint to them that even if they return in repentance, they will in any case eat the meat, for a king's decree has gone forth, supreme — but they did not understand these things, or they understood and did not justify them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:00.176068922Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}